{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'python3 .ai/scripts/repo.py read .claude/skills/implement/SKILL.md .claude/skills/spec/SKILL.md .claude/skills/plan/SKILL.md .claude/skills/review/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"==> .claude/skills/implement/SKILL.md <==\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: implement\n     3\tdescription: Use when executing an approved plan or making a bounded code change. Enforces test-first work, minimal edits, focused checks, and OKF sync.\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\tRun lifecycle Stage 2 (Implement) and Stage 4 (Verify and sync).\n     7\t\n     8\t1. Confirm the gate: `python3 .ai/scripts/lifecycle.py gate implement`. Hooks deny source edits until it passes.\n     9\t2. Load the role: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/agents/implementer.md --section Contract --section Execution` and the test rules: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show AGENTS.md --section \"8.6 Test-first and root cause\"`.\n    10\t3. Work on an isolated branch or worktree at L2 and above.\n    11\t4. Start every bug fix from a failing reproduction: `lifecycle.py redgreen red -- <test command>`. After the fix: `lifecycle.py redgreen green -- <same command>`. Write new logic test-first.\n    12\t5. Run the narrowest focused check per step. Run the slice E2E once when the slice is complete (§8.4).\n    13\t6. Finish with `okf.py affected`, the needed doc updates, and `okf.py log`. At L1–L3 close a retrospective with `lifecycle.py retrospect --lessons 1 2` for captured lessons with nonblank evidence, or `lifecycle.py retrospect --no-new-lessons REASON`. Then run `lifecycle.py stage verify --note \"<commands and outcomes>\"` after review (L2). Report the retrospective outcome at delivery; retrieved observations remain advisory.\n==> .claude/skills/spec/SKILL.md <==\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: spec\n     3\tdescription: Use before planning or coding any new or changed behavior, contract, or ambiguous request (lifecycle tier L1 and above). Clarifies the request and writes a testable spec with acceptance criteria.\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\tRun lifecycle Stage 0 (Intent).\n     7\t\n     8\tFor an existing capability, use stable namespaced IDs and a delta with target metadata, Added/Modified/Removed requirements and an approval-bound `## Target baseline` mirror/pins. Inspect the contract with `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/lifecycle.md --section \"Brownfield deltas\"`. An unstructured legacy capability needs an explicitly drafted and approved baseline first; do not infer or convert it automatically.\n     9\t\n    10\t1. Declare the tier and persona: `python3 .ai/scripts/lifecycle.py tier L2 --reason \"<why>\" --kind feature` (`AGENTS.md` §2.5).\n    11\t2. Load the stage: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/lifecycle.md --section \"Stage 0: Intent\"` and the role: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/agents/analyst.md --section Contract --section Process --section \"Spec structure\"`.\n    12\t3. At L1 write the three-line intent note and record it with `lifecycle.py stage intent --note \"<goal; success check; assumptions>\"`. At L2 and above delegate to the `analyst` subagent, relay its batched questions with recommended defaults to the user, and persist the spec with `okf.py new specifications/<slug>.md --type Specification --status draft --evidence project-documentation ...`.\n    13\t4. Run `lifecycle.py analyze knowledge/specifications/<slug>.md` and fix every Critical or High finding, then `lifecycle.py trace knowledge/specifications/<slug>.md`, then after the user approves it, `lifecycle.py stage intent --ref <spec> --approved-by human:<id>`. The approval MUST come from a user message.\n    14\t5. Do not plan or edit code until the stage is recorded.\n==> .claude/skills/plan/SKILL.md <==\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: plan\n     3\tdescription: Use after the spec is approved and before implementing a non-trivial change (lifecycle tier L1 and above). Produces a light or full implementation plan whose steps trace to spec requirements.\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\tRun lifecycle Stage 1 (Plan).\n     7\t\n     8\t1. Load the stage: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/lifecycle.md --section \"Stage 1: Plan\"` and the role: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/agents/planner.md --section Contract --section Investigation --section \"Plan size\"`.\n     9\t2. Use the light plan at L1 and the full plan at L2. Delegate L2 planning to the `planner` subagent and persist its plan.\n    10\t3. Success criteria cite spec requirement IDs; every step names its failing-closed check and, for bug fixes and new logic, the test written first.\n    11\t4. Record it: `lifecycle.py stage plan --note \"<light plan>\"` (L1) or `lifecycle.py stage plan --ref <plan>` (L2). The stage refuses a plan that omits a spec requirement ID.\n    12\t5. Do not start implementing while the plan has an open decision.\n==> .claude/skills/review/SKILL.md <==\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: review\n     3\tdescription: Use after implementing and before shipping or marking work done. Runs the independent two-pass review (spec compliance, then quality) with the completeness audit and knowledge-sync check.\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\tRun lifecycle Stage 3 (Review).\n     7\t\n     8\tFor an explicitly opted-in mechanically bound task, load `.ai/lifecycle.md` section `Mechanically bound review (opt-in)`. The parent prepares native capture before spawning the reviewer; during the interval all protected writers are frozen. The reviewer returns the full canonical report. The parent uses `lifecycle.py review save-report` and records Review from the reserved path; it must not reconstruct the report, rerun convergence or treat pending capture as approval.\n     9\t\n    10\t1. Load the stage: `python3 .ai/scripts/okf.py show .ai/lifecycle.md --section \"Stage 3: Review\"`.\n    11\t2. At L2 and above, after OKF sync run `python3 .ai/scripts/lifecycle.py converge <spec>` and fix any FAIL, then delegate to the `reviewer` subagent with the spec, plan, diff, the converge output and the other command evidence, and save its report (it starts with `# Review Report` and contains `## Verdict`; its `## Validation Reviewed` section cites the converge result and each MANUAL requirement ID). At L1 review your own diff against the same two passes.\n    12\t   Save the complete returned Markdown body unchanged, including its title and every section. Existing YAML frontmatter may hold metadata outside that body; replace any scaffold body with the returned report. Do not add a heading, introduction, summary or verdict to its body, or rewrite its content. If the handback is noncanonical, request a corrected complete report from the reviewer; the parent must not reconstruct an independent approval.\n    13\t3. Fix Critical and High findings, then re-review only the fix. Report Medium findings as fixed or accepted.\n    14\t4. Record it: `lifecycle.py stage review --note \"<self-review>\"` (L1) or `lifecycle.py stage review --ref <report>` (L2). The stage refuses a verdict that is not Approved.\n    15\t5. Check the closing retrospective before Verify: selected evidence-backed lessons or an explicit no-new-lessons reason. Capture alone does not close it. Check that delivery reports the outcome and treats retrieved observations as advisory.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
